by nicolas-grekas at 2026-07-28T04:58:05Z

Notes on what changed during review, moved out of the description so that it reads as documentation.

* **the event name was taken from an unsigned header.** The parser used to build the `RemoteEvent` from `Webhook-Event` whichever scheme had verified, so someone able to change a request in flight could relabel `payment.authorized` as `payment.refunded` and still pass. `transitional` made it worse rather than better: the parser returned on the first match, so dropping the legacy entry left only the scheme that does not cover the name. Both are closed, and a single matching entry still accepts, so rotation keeps working. Covered by a test;

* **the expected signatures are computed once instead of once per entry.** Neither expectation depends on the candidate, but both were built inside the loop, so the header size drove the CPU cost. An 8 KB `Webhook-Signature`, inside the default limits of Apache and nginx, holds about 1638 entries, and over a 512 KB body that cost 1133.7 ms per request on an unauthenticated endpoint. It is now 3.7 ms;

* entries are split on any whitespace with empty ones discarded, so a stray double space no longer costs a full HMAC each;

* **replays are bounded.** The reason the specification carries a timestamp is to limit replays, and the value was only ever used as signature input, so a captured request replayed forever. Hence `timestamp_tolerance`;

* **`whsec_` secrets interoperate.** Symfony used the configured string verbatim, so pasting a provider's `whsec_...` secret produced silent verification failures in both directions. The prefix is stripped and decoded for the Standard entry only, since decoding it for the legacy scheme would change the key for any existing deployment whose secret happens to start with those characters;

* **the format is a `SignatureFormat` enum** rather than a string with `FORMAT_*` constants, as @stof suggested. It is self-validating, so the constructor no longer has to check the value and throw, and `framework.webhook.signature_format` is mapped onto it once in the extension;

* **the receiver is wired with the configured header names.** `webhook.request_parser` was registered with no arguments, so none of `event_header_name`, `id_header_name`, `signature_header_name` or the new options reached Symfony's own receiver; renaming one made an application fail to verify its own webhooks;

* the changelog entry moved from an `8.1` heading to `8.2` and is down to two lines, with the detail listed in the description for the documentation.

