by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-11T15:07:15Z

Reworked. The receiving half did not hold up, so the scheme is now an explicit contract on both ends rather than something auto-detected per entry.

* **`timestamp_tolerance` never fired.** `HeaderSignatureConfigurator` emits the legacy entry first in `transitional`, and the parser broke out of its loop on the first legacy match, so a request signed an hour earlier was accepted. `testRejectsAReplayedStandardSignature` passed only because it hand-wrote `standard legacy`, an order the sender never produces. And since a `v1,`-only request was always rejected for not covering the event name, there was no reachable configuration in which the window had any effect;

* **the receiver could not accept a Standard Webhooks request at all.** It now can, because the event name comes from the payload's `type` key when a `v1,` entry is what authenticated, which is where the specification puts it. `JsonBodyConfigurator` fills it in on the sending side;

* **`Webhook-Event` is no longer sent in `standard`.** It was travelling unsigned. Symfony's own receiver guarded against that with an extra check; every third-party subscriber got the hole. Not sending it removes the class of bug instead of detecting it, and `nameIsSigned` along with its error message are gone;

* **the format is required, not detected.** Order-dependence disappears, `timestamp_tolerance` applies exactly where a signed timestamp exists, and each mode has one meaning on both sides;

* **`transitional` is documented for what it is.** It is not readable by a receiver older than 8.2: the pre-8.2 parser compares the whole header value with `hash_equals`, so a second entry makes it mismatch. The description no longer recommends it between Symfony applications;

* **new options no longer degrade silently.** FrameworkBundle accepts `symfony/webhook` from 7.4, and PHP drops extra constructor arguments without a word, so `signature_format: standard` on webhook 8.1 emitted legacy signatures and ignored the tolerance. Setting any of the three new options on a component older than 8.2 now throws;

* the `whsec_` handling and the Standard Webhooks payload are in one `StandardWebhooksSignature` helper instead of being duplicated in the sender and the receiver, `RequestParser` takes a clock as @andersonamuller suggested, and `symfony/clock` is declared in the component's `composer.json`;

* `Tests/SignatureRoundTripTest.php` sends through the configurators `Transport` chains and parses the result back, per format, so the replay and relabeling cases are covered against what the sender actually emits rather than against hand-built headers.

The DI wiring fix that was part of this PR went in separately as #65280 and is merged up.

